   "source": [
    "## For the baseline correction we will use the pybaseline library\n",
    "\n",
    "The pybaselines library is a Python toolkit for removing smooth background trends (baselines) from spectral data, <br> \n",
    "and it can be very useful in radio astronomy for RFI characterization. In a power spectrum, the receiver bandpass <br> \n",
    "and system effects produce a smooth baseline, while RFI appears as sharp narrow or broad spikes above it. By applying <br>\n",
    " algorithms such as Asymmetric Least Squares (AsLS), airPLS, or SNIP, one can model and subtract the baseline, leaving <br>\n",
    "  a detrended spectrum where RFI features stand out clearly. This makes it possible to apply statistical thresholds <br> \n",
    "  (e.g., sigma-clipping) to flag and count corrupted channels. In this way, pybaselines helps separate instrumental <br> \n",
    "  effects from true interference, providing a cleaner view of the spectrum and a quantitative measure of RFI contamination.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Source: https://pybaselines.readthedocs.io/en/latest/introduction.html"
   ]
  },
